    <h2>Knocked-out tooth? Time matters</h2>
    <p>An adult tooth that has been knocked out has the best chance if it is seen quickly. Pick it up by the white part, not the root. If it is dirty, rinse it briefly in milk or water. Keep it moist, in milk or tucked inside your cheek, and call us on the way.</p>
    <h2>While you wait</h2>
    <ul>
        <li>Rinse gently with warm salty water.</li>
        <li>Use a cold pack on your cheek for swelling: twenty minutes on, twenty off.</li>
        <li>Take over-the-counter pain relief as directed on the packet.</li>
        <li>Avoid very hot, cold or sweet food on that side.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2>Never just “wait and see” with swelling</h2>
    <p>Swelling in your face or gum can mean an infection that needs treating soon. Call us. If it is affecting your breathing or swallowing, go to an emergency room straight away. Our post on <a href="/blog/toothache-what-to-do-tonight/">what to do about toothache tonight</a> has more.</p>
